  body: `Prior Lessons — 1-Sentence Recap

Lesson 003: A process node such as 3nm is a manufacturing generation, and foundry economics depend heavily on process know-how and yield, not simply owning the same equipment.

Lesson 004: Chips begin on ultra-pure 300mm silicon wafers supplied by companies such as Shin-Etsu and SUMCO, showing that an input can be essential without having monopoly-like economics.

Lesson 005: A chip is built through a repeated loop of deposition, lithography, etch, doping, cleaning and inspection, so leading-edge complexity creates value for many equipment suppliers beyond ASML.

Lesson 006: Deposition adds ultra-thin films to a wafer, and increasingly three-dimensional chip structures raise the value of precise techniques such as ALD and the tools supplied by Applied Materials, Lam Research and Tokyo Electron.

Lesson 007: Etch removes selected material after lithography defines the pattern, and rising 3D complexity increases the value of high-precision plasma etch, especially for Lam Research.

The big idea

A semiconductor fab cannot wait until the end of a months-long manufacturing process to discover that a critical layer was misaligned or contaminated.

By then, hundreds of expensive process steps may already have been performed on a bad wafer.

Process control is the system of inspection, measurement and data analysis that tells the fab whether each step is actually working.

If lithography, deposition and etch are the construction crews, process control is the combination of quality-control inspector, surveyor and early-warning system.

KLA is the company most closely associated with this part of the semiconductor equipment stack.

1. Why defects are economically dangerous

Imagine a wafer containing hundreds of potential high-end chips. The wafer passes through an enormous number of fabrication steps before those chips are complete.

A defect introduced early can ruin a transistor, break an interconnect or cause a chip to fail electrical testing later.

The economic problem is not only the defect itself. It is the additional value that the fab may continue adding to a wafer that was already doomed.

That means early detection can save far more money than the cost of the inspection step.

This connects directly to yield.

Yield = the percentage of manufactured dies that work well enough to sell.

If a wafer produces 100 potential dies and 90 are usable, yield is about 90%. If only 60 are usable, a large share of the wafer-processing cost has been wasted.

For expensive AI accelerators, advanced CPUs, HBM and other leading-edge products, small changes in yield can have very large economic consequences.

2. Inspection versus metrology

These terms sound similar but answer different questions.

Inspection asks: Is something wrong?

Metrology asks: Did we build the feature to the intended dimensions and position?

Inspection looks for defects such as particles, scratches, pattern abnormalities or other unwanted features.

Metrology measures things such as:

- line width or critical dimension;
- film thickness;
- layer-to-layer alignment;
- feature shape;
- surface topography;
- material or optical properties.

A useful analogy is building a skyscraper.
